
+++++++++++++++++
Availability Set
+++++++++++++++++
The Availability Set feature in Azure Virtual Machines ensures that updates are staggered across VMs based on their update domains and fault domains. 
This mechanism is designed to maintain high availability during planned maintenance or unexpected failures.

+++++++++++++++++
Update Domains
+++++++++++++++++
Update Domains are logical groupings that define which VMs can be updated or rebooted simultaneously. 
During maintenance, Azure updates one update domain at a time, ensuring that other update domains remain operational. 
For example, if you have multiple VMs in an availability set, they are distributed across up to 20 update domains, and only one update domain is updated at a time.

+++++++++++++
Fault Domain
+++++++++++++
Fault Domains provide physical isolation by grouping VMs that share the same power source and network switch. 
This ensures that a failure in one fault domain does not impact VMs in other fault domains.

>>>>By combining update domains and fault domains, Azure minimizes downtime and ensures that applications remain available during maintenance or hardware failures.


++++++++++++
Scale Sets
++++++++++++
Scale Sets, on the other hand, are designed for scalability and automatic management of VM instances. 
They allow you to create and manage a group of load-balanced VMs that can automatically increase or decrease in response to demand or a defined schedule.

Automatic Scaling: Scale sets can automatically adjust the number of VM instances based on usage metrics or a predefined schedule.
Central Management: VM instances in a scale set can be centrally managed, configured, and updated.
Scale sets provide high availability by distributing VM instances across multiple fault domains and update domains, similar to availability sets. 
However, they also offer the flexibility to scale out or in based on demand, making them suitable for applications with varying workloads.
